%% Slow oscillation detection, delay, peak-ratio, and channel variability analysis
% This script loads the raw 90-channel EEG recording and extracts the
% predefined NREM/SWS episodes. One representative channel from each
% thalamic shank is then selected, z-score normalized, and filtered between
% 0.5 and 4 Hz for slow-oscillation analysis.
%
% Slow oscillation events are detected independently across the selected
% channels, and the first channel is used as the reference channel.
% The timing of slow-oscillation peaks across channels is compared with the
% reference events to calculate inter-channel peak delays.
%
% The mean thalamic signal around each reference slow-oscillation event is
% calculated, and the amplitude ratio and temporal delay between the
% reference peak and the subsequent peak are determined for each event.
% The distributions of peak delays, peak ratios, and reference-event
% amplitudes are visualized using scatter plots and histograms.
%
% The variability of the largest slow-oscillation peak amplitudes across
% the selected channels is quantified for each event using the mean,
% standard deviation, and coefficient of variation (CV).
%
% The mean thalamic signal is subsequently filtered between 0.5 and 10 Hz
% to repeat the peak-ratio and delay analysis using a broader frequency
% range. The same analysis is also performed specifically on the reference
% channel, and the resulting delay and ratio measures are saved for
% subsequent analyses.
%
% Finally, the peak ratios obtained from the 0.5–4 Hz and 0.5–10 Hz
% analyses, as well as those obtained from the mean signal and reference
% channel, are compared using scatter plots.

